## Summary

Recently we fixed some bugs where address, index or launch-size math was
done in 32 bits when it needed 64. That kind of bug only shows up at big
sizes or unlucky addresses, and it usually lives in a handful of
solutions out of hundreds. Our tests have a hard time reaching it,
because `unit_check` compares against an OpenBLAS GEMM on the host. That
costs M·N·K and copies every padded buffer to the host, so the big-shape
tests stay small and run only the heuristic's top pick, once.
`integer_exact` already got rid of the tolerance, but not the cost.

This PR adds fast_check, an opt-in check (`fast_check: 1`) for
`integer_exact` tests. Instead of recomputing the GEMM, it compares a
few random "fingerprints" of D with the same fingerprints computed from
the inputs (Freivalds' algorithm, in exact modular arithmetic). That
costs about as much as reading the matrices once, D never leaves the
GPU, and a second set of fingerprints pins down which elements are
wrong. Padding is filled with poison so stray reads show up, and D
starts as a sentinel so missed writes show up. Every failure names the
solution, its kernel and where each buffer sits in memory. Only test
code changes.

To make sure it catches what it's meant to, we reverted the ROCM-31016
fix (dropped stores past 2 GiB). fast_check flagged the affected
solutions and pointed at the exact element where the stores stopped. It
has also found real defects: on gfx950 the ROCM-31016 shape and the bf16
every-solution case fail today (ROCM-32152 and ROCM-32151), so those two
cases are quarantined there, and running it next to `unit_check` found a
bug in the CPU reference (ROCM-32062).

**CI time added**, measured on one MI300X / one MI350X under ROCm 10.2
at the stack tip (the sum of gtest's per-test times, not counting
test-data parsing at startup):

- This PR: about 32 s / 15 s in pre_checkin (108 cases; the 51
self-tests take under a second), and about 3.5 min / 20 s in nightly (35
/ 33 cases; gfx950 runs fewer because of the two quarantines above).
- The whole stack: about 37 s / 58 s in pre_checkin, about 15 min / 9
min in nightly, and about 3.5 min / 1 min in the new `stress` tier
(#13021), which needs large-memory runners. On MI300X the Stream-K cases
skip unless `TENSILE_SOLUTION_SELECTION_METHOD=2` is set; with it they
add about 5.5 min.

## Motivation

hipBLASLt's gtest suite checks a matrix multiply (GEMM) result with
`unit_check`, which compares the GPU output against a reference computed
on the CPU. That reference costs time proportional to M·N·K, and the
harness copies every input and output buffer, padding included, into
pinned host memory. Tests for out-of-bounds indexing need large leading
dimensions or large shapes, so this cost limits them. On gfx942,
`matmul_size_t_index_overflow` with ldd = 1049100 spends 12 s of its
14.4 s allocating host memory. The existing overflow tests therefore
keep K small and run only the heuristic's first solution, even though
indexing defects such as ROCM-31016 and ROCM-31258 affect specific
kernels.

## Technical Details

This PR adds an opt-in YAML argument, `fast_check: 1`, for tests that
use `initialization: integer_exact`. Under integer_exact, every input is
a small integer, so the correct result is an exact integer as well. Only
client test code and test data change. The library, TensileLite and
kernel selection are untouched.

- **Every element of D is checked without a CPU GEMM.** fast_check uses
Freivalds' algorithm, which multiplies D by a random vector `r` and
compares the result with `A·(B·r) + C·r + bias·sum(r)`. That costs time
proportional to M·K + K·N + M·N instead of M·N·K. It does the same from
the left with a second vector, to locate bad columns. The sums are taken
modulo the prime 2^61 − 1, so the comparison is exact, and a wrong
result passes one probe with probability below 2^−60. The input side is
computed on the host once per test. D's side is computed on the GPU for
each solution, so D is never copied to the host. When the probes
disagree, the failure message lists the mismatched elements with their
row, column and element offset, and marks offsets at or above 2^31 or
2^32.
- **Failure context.** Each failure names the solution: its position in
the test's list, its library index and its kernel. It also lists every
operand's address range and flags any that crosses a 4 GiB boundary,
since some address defects depend on placement rather than size.
- **Out-of-bounds reads.** The padding of A, B and C (rows past the
matrix inside the leading dimension, and gaps between batches) is filled
with 40, a value outside the integer_exact range [−2, 2]. A kernel that
reads padding therefore produces a wrong D.
- **Out-of-bounds writes and missed stores.** Before each solution runs,
D's whole allocation is filled with 0xFF bytes, which form a NaN in f16,
bf16 and f32. Afterwards a device kernel checks that every element
outside the M × N × batch region still holds that pattern, and that no
element inside it does. A failed launch of any of these fills or scans
fails the test; it is never read as clean.
- **Results above the exact range.** bf16 holds integers exactly only up
to 256. fast_check compares each larger result with its correctly
rounded value (round to nearest, ties to even, pinned by a self-test),
so f16 and bf16 tests can use K above the 256 limit that `unit_check`
imposes on integer_exact. Those elements are recomputed exactly, in
parallel, on the host.
- **Supported configurations:** alpha, beta, bias, `scaleAlpha_vector`,
strided batches, and every transpose, including conjugate transpose,
which stores real data the same way as transpose. An output with zero
rows, columns or batches needs no check, so it reads no operands and
launches nothing. The test fails with a stated reason for timing runs,
activations, scale factors, grouped GEMM and pointer-array batches;
later PRs in the stack lift several of these. Output types run against
real kernels here are f32, f16 and bf16; the stack adds int8 and fp8
cases (#13013, #13022). The code also accepts f64 and fp8 outputs, which
only the self-tests exercise.

New files are `clients/common/include/fast_check.hpp`,
`clients/common/src/fast_check.cpp` and
`clients/tests/src/fast_check_gtest.cpp`. `testing_matmul.hpp` calls
them, and in fast_check-only runs it skips the CPU reference and the
padded host copies.

Running both checks on the same results found ROCM-32062, a separate
defect in the CPU reference. When `scaleAlpha_vector` is combined with a
leading dimension of A larger than its row count, `cast_mul` in
`cblas_interface.cpp` scales the wrong rows of A, so `unit_check` fails
a correct GPU result. No existing test combined the two. That case,
`matmul_fast_check_epilogue_padded_lda_ROCM32062`, is quarantined in
`known_bugs.yaml` until the fix PR removes the entry; #13015 adds a
fast_check-only copy that keeps checking the GPU there.

Related: ROCM-26455, ROCM-31016 (#12281), ROCM-31230 (#12332),
ROCM-31258 (#12482), and the original overflow tests (#8231).

## Test Plan

- `FastCheck` (host only) and `FastCheckDevice` (small device buffers,
no hipBLASLt kernels) in `fast_check_gtest.cpp`. These build correct
results, inject faults, and check that each fault is reported: a wrong
element, a pair of errors that cancel in a plain row sum, a shifted row,
a duplicated column, an unwritten element, an out-of-bounds write, wrong
rounding of a large bf16 result (including a tie), an empty buffer, an
empty output (zero M, N or batch count, with null unused operands), and
an unsupported type. They also require the device and host passes to
reach the same verdict.
- pre_checkin, 126 cases: fast_check and `unit_check` on the same
results across precisions and transposes, including all nine N/T/C pairs
on two rectangular f32 shapes with padded leading dimensions and three
batches; the size_t index-overflow shapes in f32, f16 and bf16; and the
ROCM-26455 shape with bias and `scaleAlpha_vector`.
- nightly, 35 cases: element offsets and a batch base offset above 2^32;
shapes up to 16385 × 16383 × 4097 with partial tiles; and every solution
at the output-overflow and ROCM-31016 shapes.
- Regression replay: revert the ROCM-31016 threshold and run
`matmul_fast_check_store_extent_ROCM31016`.
- `standard` tier (smoke, quick, pre_checkin) to confirm existing tests
are unchanged.

